Q: Use zstd compression for a client with Ktor A: <p>There's no shortcut in the Ktor client to enable <code>ContentEncoding</code> with <code>zstd</code>, like there is for <code>gzip</code> or <code>deflate</code></p>
<p>But you can register a custom encoder for zstd like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import io.ktor.client.plugins.compression.ContentEncoding
import io.ktor.encoding.zstd.ZstdEncoder

val client = HttpClient(CIO) {
    install(ContentEncoding) {
        customEncoder(ZstdEncoder())
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Make sure you include the dependency <code>io.ktor:ktor-client-encoding</code> in your build files to have access to the <code>customEncoder</code> and <code>io.ktor:ktor-encoding-zstd</code> for the actual <code>ZstdEncoder</code>.</p>

Q: How to change Froala editor usage from jQuery to vanilla JavaScript? A: <p><strong>Disclaimer</strong>: As pointed out by VLAZ in comments, given the code you shared, it's possible you are using an older version of FroalaEditor that requires jQuery to work. In that case you should follow his suggestion to migrate to the latest version. My answer refers to the docs talking about the latest version being froala_editor v5.0.1. That's true also with the working demo that's using assets from the latest version and it wouldn't work, as that, with the v2.9 for example.</p>
<p>You said that you are struggling translating the following line:</p>
<pre><code>SB.Append(&quot;$('#synopsis').froalaEditor('html.set', `&quot; &amp; vSynopis &amp; &quot;`); } &quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>To something appending a vanilla javascript line that won't make use of jQuery.</p>
<p>To be fair jQuery is involved two times there: when selecting the <code>#synopsis</code> element; and when invoking the <code>froalaEditor</code> method over the jQuery object returned by the <code>$('#..')</code> document selector.</p>
<p>If you intend to strip off jQuery entirely you'll need to initialize the Froala editor differently upfront and then later calling <code>html.set</code> over the editor object obtained before.</p>
<p>To initialize the editor you will need to use <code>new FroalaEditor()</code>:</p>
<p><a href="https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/activation/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/activation/</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>const editor = new FroalaEditor('your_selector', {key: &quot;ACTIVATION_KEY&quot;})</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And to <em>Replace the whole document with new content</em>:</p>
<p><a href="https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/migration-guides/tiptap/methods/#replace-the-whole-document-with-new-content" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/migration-guides/tiptap/methods/#replace-the-whole-document-with-new-content</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>editor.html.set('hey, that’s cool!');</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In short that would become:</p>
<pre><code>var editor = new FroalaEditor('#synopsis');
editor.html.set('&lt;p&gt;Hello from backend!&lt;/p&gt;');
</code></pre>
<pre><code>SB.Append(&quot;var editor = new FroalaEditor('#synopsis');&quot;)
SB.Append(&quot;editor.html.set('&quot; &amp; vSynopis &amp; &quot;');&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>Side notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>you used a different element id in your attempt (<code>#query</code> instead of <code>#synopsis</code>)</li>
<li>the line you are struggling to translate has a closing <code>}</code> I removed because it's not clear to which block is referring to</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a working demo showing the concept:</p>
<p>*<strong>it's using a trick to bypass the license that wouldn't be needed if we used jquery instead... it was done for the sake of demonstration purpose inside the live snippet sandbox and nothing more</strong></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var editor = new FroalaEditor('#synopsis');

function setContent() {
  editor.html.set('&lt;p&gt;Hello from backend!&lt;/p&gt;');
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#setContent{
  margin-bottom: 1em;
  padding: 1em;
  cursor: pointer;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!--
  this is a trick to prevent the cookie check for licensing purpose
  that would make this demo not working otherwise
--&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
  try {
    Object.defineProperty(document, 'cookie', {
      set: function() {},
      get: function() { return ''; }
    });
  } catch(e) {}
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!-- froala editor assets --&gt;
&lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;button id="setContent" onclick="setContent()"&gt;Set Content&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;div id="synopsis"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Original content&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<hr />
<p>Now since you are opening a third party page you can't control, I would bet that it's now using a newer version of Froala and it dismissed jQuery entirely, since you said it's no more available. This wouldn't prevent you to inject jQuery on your own, if you intended to use jQuery code to select elements. But yet it's not needed because what you are trying to achieve is just changing the content of arbitrary fields powered by the Froala editor.</p>
<p>So how to proceed from there?</p>
<p>You may first of all, find out which version of Froala Editor is being used just for the sake of curiosity, by running code from a javascript console on that same web page opened in a regular browser:</p>
<p><a href="https://wysiwyg-editor.froala.help/hc/en-us/articles/360000717049-How-can-I-find-my-editor-version" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://wysiwyg-editor.froala.help/hc/en-us/articles/360000717049-How-can-I-find-my-editor-version</a></p>
<pre><code>$.FE.VERSION //Version 2.x
FroalaEditor.VERSION //Version 3.x and later
</code></pre>
<p>Example showing Froala Editor version for 2.*
<div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>console.log(FroalaEditor.VERSION)</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  try {
    Object.defineProperty(document, 'cookie', {
      set: function() {},
      get: function() { return ''; }
    });
  } catch(e) {}
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!-- froala-editor@5.0.1 assets --&gt;
&lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.0.1/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.0.1/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Example showing Froala Editor version for &gt;= 3.*
<div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>console.log($.FE.VERSION)</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  try {
    Object.defineProperty(document, 'cookie', {
      set: function() {},
      get: function() { return ''; }
    });
  } catch(e) {}
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/1.11.0/jquery.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!-- froala-editor@2.9.0 assets --&gt;
&lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@2.9.0/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@2.9.0/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<hr />
<p>By the way what you need now is how to get the editor object using the id selector, being the only information you have to retrieve the target field and that so far was <code>#synopsis</code>.</p>
<p>In previous versions of Froala you could use the <code>froalaEditor()</code> method over the jQuery object fetched via selector but with vanilla javascript the only way I found was here <a href="https://wysiwyg-editor.froala.help/hc/en-us/articles/360000363629-How-can-I-get-the-editor-instance" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://wysiwyg-editor.froala.help/hc/en-us/articles/360000363629-How-can-I-get-the-editor-instance</a></p>
<pre><code>$('.selector')[0]['data-froala.editor']
</code></pre>
<p>So this is the final solution:</p>
<pre><code>document.querySelector('#synopsis')['data-froala.editor'].html.set('your custom content')
</code></pre>
<pre><code>SB.Append(&quot;document.querySelector('#synopsis')['data-froala.editor'].html.set('&quot; &amp; vSynopis &amp; &quot;');&quot;)
</code></pre>
